                    briefing_text_r2:       `You have a <strong>₺60M change budget</strong> and only <strong>10 units of manager attention</strong>. Don't pile every lever on at once — supervisors have no bandwidth to coach any of it and none lands. Choose how much to launch, then allocate the budget across <strong>delegation</strong> (line-stop authority for quality), <strong>voice</strong> (daily huddles & a no-blame escape channel) and <strong>recognition</strong>. The audit says <em>silence</em> caused the escape, so voice is the central lever. Finally, frame the change: empowerment AS paternalist care raises voice while honouring hierarchy; a mandate buys paper compliance; a Western flat model dishonours the supervisors and tanks Cultural-Fit.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Manage resistance & a live test`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `A worker used the new line-stop authority over a minor issue — a two-hour stoppage for a false alarm. The veteran Operations Lead, who runs 40% of the floor, demands you revoke it and “restore order.” <strong>Retract it and the message on the floor is: speaking up gets punished</strong> — and the Empowerment Index falls back to where it started. Simultaneously the German customer requests a plant visit in two weeks. Decide how to respond to the incident, how to turn the resistant power-holder from blocker into <em>owner</em>, what to honestly show the customer, and how hard to protect frontline and coaching time so the change actually embeds.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the transformation`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the board chair and the German supplier-quality director. Commit to a <strong>90-day proof plan</strong>: name your <strong>leading and lagging indicators</strong>, say who sustains empowerment once your attention moves on, and be honest about pacing. Culture change is months, not weeks — <em>declare it “transformed” in eight weeks</em> and they audit for evidence you can't show, and you lose trust on the next audit. Show that empowerment was <strong>adapted to</strong>, not imposed on, the Turkish context, and that hierarchy and voice now coexist.`,

                    r1_root_label:          `The audit says workers saw the defect and stayed silent. What is the root cause?`,
                    r1_norm_label:          `Which local norm must any empowerment move work WITH, not against?`,
                    r1_link_label:          `How clearly does your team link the control culture to the quality escape (not 'more tools')? (%)`,
                    r1_link_readout:        `Diagnosis that voice — not checklists — fixes silence:`,
                    root_control:           `The over-controlled culture — workers can't decide or speak up, so problems escalate slowly and silently`,
                    root_quality:           `A quality-tools gap — more inspections and a stricter defect checklist will fix it`,
                    root_training:          `A training gap — workers were never taught to recognise this defect`,
                    root_individuals:       `Careless individuals on the line who didn't do their job`,
                    norm_paternalism:       `Paternalist care & respect for hierarchy — reframe empowerment AS the boss caring, not abandoning`,
                    norm_flat:              `None — announce a flat, speak-your-mind culture on day one and remove the supervisor layer`,
                    norm_pay:               `Only pay matters — raise wages and the voice problem solves itself`,
                    norm_seniority:         `Pure seniority — let the most senior worker always decide, regardless of knowledge`,

                    r2_load_label:          `Manager attention is limited (10 units). How many empowerment levers do you launch at once?`,
