                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The workers don't lack tools — they lack the safety and the authority to act on what they already saw. Fix the control culture and the quality escape and the talent bleed close together.`,
                    coach_r1_quality:       `<strong>You're tooling the wrong problem.</strong> More inspections and a stricter checklist? The audit said workers <em>saw</em> the defect and stayed silent — no checklist fixes silence. What leadership behaviour, not what tool, makes a worker confident enough to stop the line?`,
                    coach_r1_training:      `<strong>More training won't reach this.</strong> They already recognised the defect — they just didn't feel able to raise it. This is a voice and authority problem, not a knowledge problem.`,
                    coach_r1_individuals:   `<strong>Blaming individuals is the comfortable answer.</strong> When a whole floor stays silent, that is the culture working as designed, not careless people. Diagnose the control system, not the worker.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a control-culture problem AND located the lever in paternalist care and respect for hierarchy. That is exactly how you raise voice without dishonouring the supervisors your people respect.`,
                    coach_r1_flat:          `<strong>You announced a flat, speak-your-mind culture on day one.</strong> The young cell loved it; the veteran lines went silent and your Cultural-Fit dropped hard because workers felt you'd dishonoured the supervisors they respect. Empowerment isn't the absence of hierarchy — how do you raise voice <em>while</em> honouring it?`,
                    coach_r1_pay:           `<strong>Pay alone won't buy voice.</strong> A raise is welcome, but it doesn't make a worker feel safe to stop the line. The lever is psychological safety inside the existing relationships of care and respect.`,
                    coach_r1_seniority:     `<strong>Seniority isn't the same as knowledge.</strong> Letting the most senior person always decide keeps the decision away from the worker who can actually see the defect. Match the decision to where the knowledge is.`,
                    coach_r1_lowlink:       `<strong>You're hedging on the diagnosis.</strong> If you won't clearly say that silence — not a checklist gap — caused this, you'll quietly drift back to quality tools and repeat the escape.`,
                    coach_r2_sequenced:     `<strong>Two levers, sequenced — that's the disciplined choice.</strong> Supervisors only have so much bandwidth; picking the two that change behaviour before the visit and embedding them beats launching everything and landing nothing.`,
                    coach_r2_overload:      `<strong>Delegation, voice, recognition, huddles — all in week one, on top of the day job.</strong> Supervisors have no bandwidth to coach any of it, so none lands. Which two moves, sequenced, would actually change behaviour before the customer visit?`,
                    coach_r2_one:           `<strong>One lever is too thin.</strong> A single move won't shift a deeply controlled floor in eight weeks. You have bandwidth for two — use it deliberately.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>Voice leads, delegation gives it teeth, recognition makes it stick.</strong> Funding the no-blame channel and huddles so problems surface, backing them with real line-stop authority, and rewarding the workers who use it — that is a coherent empowerment package.`,
                    coach_r2_novoice:       `<strong>You've starved voice.</strong> The audit said the defect escaped because workers stayed silent — under-funding the huddles and the no-blame channel leaves the root cause untouched. Voice is the central lever here.`,
                    coach_r2_nodelegation:  `<strong>Voice without authority is just talk.</strong> If workers can surface a problem but still can't stop the line, you've raised expectations you can't honour. Delegation gives voice its teeth.`,
                    coach_r2_care:          `<strong>Framing empowerment as the firm's care is the cultural key.</strong> “We trust and protect you to stop a bad part” raises voice within respected hierarchy — paternalism reframed, not abandoned.`,
                    coach_r2_mandate:       `<strong>Mandate-and-audit buys attendance, not belief.</strong> Order the huddles and audit them hard and people show up and stay silent — the escape happens again the week you look away.`,
                    coach_r2_western:       `<strong>“No more bosses, everyone's equal now” lands badly here.</strong> It reads as dishonouring the supervisors your people respect, and your Cultural-Fit pays for it. Raise voice <em>through</em> the hierarchy, not by abolishing it.`,
                    coach_r3_reinforce:     `<strong>You backed the worker who stopped the line — that's the move.</strong> Early empowerment requires tolerating an imperfect first use. The two-hour stoppage was cheap tuition; teaching the floor that voice is safe is priceless.`,
                    coach_r3_retract:       `<strong>A worker spoke up, so you pulled the authority.</strong> Message received on the floor: “speaking up gets punished.” Your Empowerment Index just fell back toward where you started. Was the two-hour stoppage really more expensive than re-teaching everyone that voice is unsafe?`,
                    coach_r3_quiet:         `<strong>Quietly discouraging it is retraction with deniability.</strong> Workers read the signal instantly and stop using the authority. You keep the policy on paper and lose it in practice.`,
                    coach_r3_owner:         `<strong>You turned your veteran from victim into owner.</strong> Giving him a visible role designing the line-stop rules honours his authority and experience — a program the middle layer owns is real change, not theatre.`,
                    coach_r3_overrule:      `<strong>Making an example of the veteran is a short-term win and a long-term wound.</strong> He runs 40% of the floor; humiliating him hardens his resistance and reads as dishonour to everyone who respects him. Make him an owner instead.`,
                    coach_r3_bypass:        `<strong>Routing around the veteran leaves a saboteur in charge of 40% of the floor.</strong> A program the middle layer resents is theatre. You have to bring the power-holder along, not pretend he isn't there.`,
                    coach_r3_transformed:   `<strong>You told the German director the culture is “transformed.”</strong> Two weeks in? They audit for real evidence and won't find it — and you've spent trust you'll need on the next visit. Show honest early behaviours and a credible plan instead.`,
                    coach_r3_hide:          `<strong>Staging the visit and hiding the night shift is a trust bomb.</strong> Supplier-quality directors are paid to find the gap between the showroom and the floor. Honesty about what's in progress earns more credit than a polished cover.`,
                    coach_r3_noprotect:     `<strong>You've asked for change with no protected time.</strong> Telling supervisors to coach huddles and workers to add steps with no backfill produces resentment and quiet non-compliance, not adoption. Protect the time you're asking them to spend.`,
                    coach_r4_leadlag:       `<strong>Leading plus lagging indicators is the right spine.</strong> Escapes caught and huddle decisions in real time tell you it's working before the defect-cost and attrition data confirm it — and prove the change is real, not a paper claim.`,
                    coach_r4_lagonly:       `<strong>Lagging-only is too slow.</strong> Quarterly defect cost satisfies a report but won't warn you when the behaviours decay — by the time the number moves, the floor has already drifted back.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>A launch photo and a survey spike is not measurement.</strong> That's exactly the decay pattern: attention moves on and the behaviours quietly die. Measure the behaviours that have to outlive your attention.`,
                    coach_r4_central:       `<strong>If it only works while you chair every huddle, it isn't sustainable.</strong> The moment your attention moves, it decays. Coach the supervisors to own it where the work happens.`,
                    coach_r4_nobody:        `<strong>“Momentum will carry it” is how the old culture wins back.</strong> Without named owners and a place in induction and handover, the floor slides back to silent escalation the week you look away.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest answer.</strong> Early behaviours real and rising, supervisors owning it, leading indicators in place, and an honest 90-day plan — that is a transformation that survives, and a customer relationship that survives the next audit.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You promised a finished transformation in eight weeks.</strong> Culture change is months, not weeks, and the customer audits for evidence. Overclaim now and the next audit catches you short — honest pacing protects the relationship.`,
                    coach_r4_coerce:        `<strong>Ordering compliance and auditing hard gets you a number, not a culture.</strong> People perform the behaviours while you watch and revert when you don't — and the dishonour of the mandate corrodes the very trust empowerment needs.`,

